From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Scott Smith <scott@newtrostudios.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 18:36:34 -0400
Subject: remotesmith(0104): first-run provisioning (server, LAN only or
 controller only; password)

On first launch the desktop home page shows a setup dialog until the machine
has a route and, if it accepts incoming connections, a permanent password:
- route: ID server host + public key (validated: host[:port] or
  [IPv6]:port, 44-char base64 Ed25519 key), or paste an exported server
  config, or "Direct LAN only" (remotesmith-lan-only=Y). A host without a
  valid key does not count.
- "Controller only": the machine never accepts incoming connections, using
  upstream's stop-service option (no rendezvous registration, no direct-IP
  listener), and needs no password. Settings > General > Start service
  turns hosting on, and the dialog then asks for the password.
- otherwise a permanent password of at least 12 characters, with a
  generator (20 chars from the OS CSPRNG, no look-alikes, always a digit,
  lower and upper case).
Values go through the same option/password calls as Settings, so they are
stored in upstream's config (password hashed/encrypted as upstream does).
Nothing is prefilled; an existing relay/API server is kept.

Direct LAN only is enforced, not just recorded: hbb_common 0104 drops every
rendezvous source, and here the API server, the exe-name server, id@server
connections and get_custom_rendezvous_server() return nothing. Choosing a
server (dialog, Settings, CLI, --config) turns LAN only off; choosing LAN
only (dialog, CLI) clears the stored servers.

Headless: --option custom-rendezvous-server/key/remotesmith-lan-only/
stop-service and --password apply the same validation before anything is
written; --config and --import-config refuse a malformed or key-less
server. The Settings password dialog minimum goes from 8 to 12. Logic and
tests live in src/remotesmith.rs; four small FFI functions expose it.

Plan: FLOW-2, DEC-10, DEF-2, AC-40, AC-10.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
